<p>When I was in Los Angeles <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/quick-thoughts-about-the-new-macbook-pro/">getting briefed</a> on the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/macbook-pro-with-proled-review-you-could-even-say-it-glows/">MacBook Pro with PROLED</a>, I asked Apple representatives directly about how they made the Apple logo on the back glow again, given that OLED displays don&#8217;t leak light out the back.</p>
<p>They jokingly told me it was &#8220;magic,&#8221; said that it was actually hard work to do, and refused to get into details.</p>
<p>Okay, I said—I&#8217;ll just wait for <a href="https://www.ifixit.com/News/120152/we-tore-down-the-macbook-proled-xdr-and-found-the-secret-of-the-glowing-apple">iFixit to tear one apart</a>.</p>
<blockquote><p>
  On old MacBooks, light from the LCD backlight shone through an apple-shaped window in the lid. But the PROLED’s pixels make their own light. So, yes, Apple waited until the first MacBook Pro without a backlight to bring back the backlit logo.</p>
<p>  Of course we had to get inside.
</p></blockquote>
<p>The result is pretty much what you&#8217;d expect:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Under a taped patch in the middle we found four LEDs, all in a strip along one edge. The light hits a reflective layer and passes through a diffuser, turning four little points of light into one evenly glowing apple. The logo itself has a mirror-like coating that looks shiny with the lights off.
</p></blockquote>
<p>(There&#8217;s also a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xJ_wUtbxUyY">YouTube video</a> of the teardown.)</p>
